The test host is ~5.7 min in, on 4 cores. 11k tests unsharded on 4 cores — could easily be 1-2 hours. This is a long wait, but the deliverable depends on it.

Let me think about whether there's a smarter approach: the CI's own test-core runs `dart test` SHARDED ×4 (test/core+shared files only). The PR's own description verify used bare `dart test` and completed (the story machine reported +10984 passed) — so bare dart test does complete on a machine like this, just slowly.

While waiting, I'll now write the review-thread reply for the stale guard-test comment fix I've planned (post-suite), then keep wide-spaced checks. Actually — better idea for the stale comment: instead of editing AFTER the suite (creating a post-suite diff), I can PRE-STAGE nothing... no, any edit now stales the run.

Alternatively, reconsider: is the guard-test comment really worth the post-suite edit dance? Let me re-read it:

```
      // The check runs a REPO script — a hosted runner's empty workspace
      // has no scripts/ (the first cut had NO checkout: exit 127 reddened
      // the pre-check on every arm and cascaded into a red Quality gate,
      // runs 38696670029/38696668627). A step must provide the file, and
      // it must run on every arm that can reach the check step (the
      // release-pending pattern: the fetch is gated to the arm that
      // needs it, other arms keep paying nothing). The fetch is shallow +
      // blobless — seconds inside the 5-minute job budget; the lookup
      // itself is still ONE gh run list API call.
```

"(the release-pending pattern: the fetch is gated to the arm that needs it, other arms keep paying nothing)" — flatly contradicts the `isNull` pin below it. Yes, worth fixing. It's 2 lines; post-suite targeted re-verification is proportionate.

Keep waiting. Wide-spaced checks from here — I'll count the suite host process growth as the progress signal.

